# Lesson 3, new exercise: draw trumps before cashing dummy's diamonds, then throw a loser on them.
title: Trumps first, then diamonds
id: L03-08
type: exercise
lesson: 3
concepts: 3.3
student: S
board: 40
dealer: W
vulnerable: none

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  K82    K964   AKQ7   53
  E  9653   73     T4     AKJ96
  S  A74    AQJ85  62     Q72
  W  QJT    T2     J9853  T84

auction:
  W Pass
  N 1D     = 13+ points, at least four diamonds
  E Pass
  S 1H*    = 6+ points, at least four hearts
     not *: With 6+ points and a 4-card major higher than partner's suit, what does the lesson respond?
  W Pass
  N 2H     = Four hearts, a minimum opening
  E Pass
  S 4H*    = Game
     key: 14 points with the doubleton, and partner opened: at least 13. That's 27. Bid game.
     not Pass: Did you add it up? Partner opened, so 14 plus 13 is already past 25.
     not 3H: Can your side have less than 25? Partner opened, so you have at least 27.
     not *: Partner opened and has four hearts. Can you see 25 points?
  W Pass
  N Pass
  E Pass

auction recap:
  Partner opened 1♦ and raised your hearts. With 14 points opposite an
  opening bid, you bid game. You are declarer, because you bid hearts first,
  and so West will lead to the first trick.

planning:
  trumps out: 4
  losers: 3
  spades: 1 = The third spade. It may go away on dummy's extra diamond.
  hearts: 0 = Solid.
  diamonds: 0 = Dummy has them covered.
  clubs: 2 = The ace and king. Dummy can ruff the third.
  remind: You can afford three losers in 4♥. Count the obvious losers suit by suit in your own hand.
  remind: Dummy has more diamonds than you. What could you do with the extra one? And what could go wrong before trumps are drawn?
play:
  1   W SQ  N S2  E S3  S SA
      note: Four losers: one spade and three clubs. Dummy's third diamond can take care of one of them. But first, draw trumps, or an opponent with short diamonds could ruff it.
  2   S H5  W H2  N HK  E H3
      note: Draw trumps.
  3   N H4  E H7  S HA  W HT
      note: Both opponents followed twice. Trumps are drawn.
  4   S D2  W D3  N DA  E D4
      note: Now the diamonds.
  5   N DK  E DT  S D6  W D5
      note: The ♦K.
  6   N DQ  E S5  S S4  W D8
      note: East has no diamonds left, but no trumps either. Throw your spade loser away.
  7   N SK  E S6  S S7  W ST
      note: Cash the ♠K.
  8   N S8  E S9  S H8  W SJ
      note: You have no spades left, so ruff dummy's last spade.
      ok: D
  9   S C2  W C4  N C3  E CJ
      note: Now the clubs. You have two losers there, but dummy can ruff the third round.
  10  E CA  S C7  W C8  N C5
  11  E CK  S CQ  W CT  N H6
      note: Dummy ruffs.
  12  N D7  E C9  S HQ  W D9
      note: Dummy's last diamond. You ruff it in your hand.
  13  S HJ  W DJ  N H9  E C6
      note: 11 tricks: 4♥ made with an overtrick.

play recap:
  The plan: draw trumps, then throw your spade loser on dummy's third
  diamond, and ruff the third club in dummy.

  Drawing trumps first mattered. If you had played diamonds at once, East
  would have ruffed the ♦Q, and your spade loser would still be there.
